the republic a book that is considered  plato's magnus opus it is an attempt by  plato to define the ideal state and what  an attempt that was in 10 magnificent  chapters the philosopher manages to  immerse ourselves in dialogues  illuminating cognitive brilliance and in  philosophical concepts of the greatest  importance all of plato's books have  more or less the same format socrates is  the main hero so to speak and he has  dialogues with different athenians since  plato and socrates both lived in athens  and teached philosophy there the main  theme of the book is that of justice  with plato's background and experience  living in athens during the time of the  30 tyrants after the peloponnesian war  many people supposed that his experience  triggered the inception of the republic  whatever the case plato created a  philosophical work of extraordinary  significance that manages to inspire  western thought till this very day  why do people demand justice  why is justice at the epicenter of our  experience  is it because we fear social punishment  or is it something more visceral that  emerges from our desire to cooperate and  co-exist according to plato justice is  worthwhile in and of itself he begins to  support this thesis in a very  interesting way he introduces us to  thrassi mahouse a sophist a teacher for  hire for the wealthy sons of athens  sophists believe in subjective morality  or moral relativism as we call it today  they don't set them actions as right or  wrong but rather as advantageous or  disadvantageous for the people  performing them  tracima who's during a discussion with  socrates asserts that justice is nothing  else than the interest of the stronger  by that he means that the government is  concerned with justice and that people  should just follow the rules of the  government and sokratis refutes his  argument in a quite lengthy but in my  opinion genius way by explaining him how  governments can make errors then tracy  mahouse posits that injustice is better  than justice because the strong realize  how to take advantage of erroneous  systems to promote their interests  socrates clearly disagrees with that and  again refutes the argument in a lengthy  but genius way he proposes to look for  justice in the city first and then to  proceed by analogy to find justice  inside the individual plato from this  deep discussion on justice tries to  extrapolate what the ideal republic can  look like political justice is  predicated upon harmony on the way  society is structured an ideal state  consists of three main classes of people  rulers warriors and craftsmen the rulers  are philosophers who can reason and  think deeply and provide a good ruling  system for the city the warriors guard  the city from internal and external  threats the craftsmen are mainly  producers and provide the city with the  necessary products and services required  to run properly the reason plato decides  to use these three categories is because  he believes that people have three  fundamental behavioral tendencies  appetite spirit and reason and that in  every person one of the three tends to  prevail the ones who gravitate towards  appetite should become craftsmen  the ones in whom the spirit prevails  should become warriors and the ones who  hold the reason as the ultimate virtue  in life should become rulers after  rigorous selection and long periods of  training a society is just when  relations between these three classes  are balanced sokratis doesn't really  explain how the classification will  ensue but he suggests that all the  citizens should be told a noble lie  called the myth of the metals so as to  promote allegiance to the state and  enforce its three-tiered social order  according to this myth of metals every  citizen is born out of the earth of the  state and every other citizen is his  brother or sister yet god has created  them differently mixing different metals  into their soul  gold for the rulers silver for the  warriors and brass or iron for the  craftsmen children are usually made of  the same metal as their parents but if  this is not the case the child must  either descend or ascend in the social  order  mentioned before a society is just when  relations between these three classes  are balanced this is a direct parallel  to the human soul plato believes that  individual justice expresses itself  through the inner balance of the soul  its individual soul is affected by three  separate parts the appetite part lasts  after money food  drink and sex the spirit part yearns for  honor the rational part desires truth  and knowledge in essence we all have a  craftsman a warrior and a philosopher  ruler inside us  in a just so the power dynamics between  these three parts are specific the  rational part rules the spirit part  enforces the rational parts convictions  and the appetite part needs to operate  according to the directions of the  former two  plato realizes that if the rational part  doesn't rule the whole constitution of  the individual can fall apart he also  believes that the just soul strives  wholly towards the truth the idea of  truth is emblematic of his work and it  becomes apparent in the allegory of the  cave the cave symbolizes this limited  world that keeps us trapped and the  shadows are the only things we perceive  as real because of the limitations  imposed upon us when the prisoner  escapes and gains access to the outer  world he encounters the sun which is the  absolute truth plato makes three  important points in the allegory with  regards to the truth one that it takes  time to get accustomed to the truth and  eventually re-engineer one's perception  in order to understand it and accept it  number two that once one sees the truth  and accepts it one can never go back to  their former state and number three that  the truth can be perceived as something  dangerous by some people because often  the truth hurts so much that people will  feel that it's not worth risking what  they have for something so precarious  the manifestation of the truth-seeking  individual is the philosopher king  that's why he writes until philosophers  are kings or the kings and princes of  this world have the spirit and power of  philosophy and political greatness and  wisdom meeting one cities will never  have rest from their evils no nor the  human race as i believe and then only  will this state have a possibility of  life and behold the light of day i'm not  sure if the republic really constitutes  an attempt from plato to create the  ideal state or if it is an allegory and  a critique towards different forms of  government i'm on the same page with  plato with regards to the idea that we  need more philosophers in politics but  i'm not sure i agree with the rest  philosophy allows the thinking human to  study the greatest problems of mankind  in depth politicians don't do that  they're just actors in a flawed and  mostly corrupt system but a few of them  have actually studied philosophy and the  ones that did mainly scratch the surface  the difficulties of political life are  inescapable and require honest and  responsible participation plato believes  in hierarchies and propaganda and that  is very dangerous he tries to create an  elitist society both intellectually and  hierarchically obviously with good  intentions but it risks the fact that  this regime can easily regress into  authoritarianism people that don't  belong in the higher classes will always  feel inferior and grow resent towards  the other classes resentment is a very  dangerous feeling democracy is certainly  flawed and prone to manipulation but it  just state cares about the well-being of  all citizens without discrimination  intellectual development should be the  bedrock of all citizens and should  encourage people to participate and feel  included especially when it comes to  small-scale experiments and governance  in any case the republic is a seminal  work filled with great thought  experiments orchestrated by a genius  format called socratic dialogue plato is  one of the pioneers of western thought  and he will always fill our minds and  souls with innumerable moments of  inspiration and awe  hey guys hope you enjoyed today's video  if you did make sure to like subscribe  turn on notifications and comment below  something cool please so that more  people can discover it  if you want to watch more videos from my  channel check out this one and this one  take care see you soon either now
 

